{
  "title": "Unique Paths",
  "description": "A robot starts in the top-left cell of a grid with `m` rows and `n` columns. On each move it steps one cell **right** or one cell **down**.\n\nReturn the number of different paths the robot can take to reach the bottom-right cell.\n\n### Constraints\n\n- `1 <= m, n <= 100`\n- The answer is at most `2000000000`.\n- Inputs are passed as function arguments and the answer is returned from the function. Do not read from standard input or print the result.\n",
  "reference": "int unique_paths(int m, int n) {\n    vector<long long> row(n, 1);\n    for (int r = 1; r < m; ++r) {\n        for (int c = 1; c < n; ++c) {\n            row[c] += row[c - 1];\n        }\n    }\n    return (int)row[n - 1];\n}\n",
  "template": "int unique_paths(int m, int n) {\n    // Return the number of right/down paths through an m by n grid.\n    return 0;\n}\n",
  "tags": [
    "Math",
    "Dynamic Programming"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "test_number": 0,
      "explanation": "Every path makes 2 moves down and 6 moves right, 8 moves in total. Choosing which 2 of the 8 moves go down can be done in 28 ways."
    },
    {
      "test_number": 1,
      "explanation": "The robot needs 2 moves down and 1 move right. The right move can come first, second, or last, which gives 3 paths."
    }
  ],
